course: dictionary
lesson: 5258
title: Jolly: one word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Jolly has more than one meaning, so context tells you which one to use. «The jolly sailor sang all day on the deck.» Here jolly describes a person who is happy, cheerful and friendly.
explain: «Jolly good!» means something different. In British talk, jolly means very, so jolly good means very good. Ask yourself: does happy fit here, or does very fit better? The words around it and the speaker's mood will show you.
explain: «Why not stay on quietly here, like us, and be jolly?» Read it as a friendly invitation to be happy and enjoy yourself. Try swapping in happy and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Jolly is a word with several meanings, so always check the context.
fact: «Jolly» means happy, cheerful and friendly, as in a jolly sailor or a jolly Father Christmas.
fact: In British talk, «jolly good» and «jolly well» mean very good and very well.
fact: To jolly someone along (informal) means to cheer them up to keep them going.
fact: A «jolly» (UK, informal) is a trip or outing for fun.
exercise: Father Christmas is described as jolly in a story. What does jolly mean here? | *Happy, cheerful and friendly | Cold and windy | Sad and tired | Very, as in very good
exercise: «Jolly good!» said Mum when I cleaned my room. What does jolly mean in this phrase? | Lost | Angry | Heavy | *Very
exercise: Rory says, «Let's go on a jolly to the seaside!» What is a jolly here? | *A trip or outing for fun | A type of boat | A rainy day | A grumpy old man
test: Toad says, «Me stop indoors and write a lot of rotten letters on a jolly morning like this.» Which meaning fits best for jolly here? | Very angry | Lost at sea | Very, very quiet | *Bright, pleasant and happy
test: Her friends tried to jolly her along after the race. What does jolly along mean here? | Race her home | Tell her off | Pull her over the line | *Cheer her up to keep her going
exam: Which sentence uses jolly to mean very? | «The jolly sailor waved at the crowd.» | «The jolly children laughed all afternoon.» | *«Jolly good, you finished the puzzle!» | «Let's go on a jolly to the park.»
